{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders","id":79886,"title":"On playing oposite genders","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-25T19:53:00-08:00","id":79886,"title":"On playing oposite genders","content":"This <span style=\"color:#444444\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Thursday for the first time in 13 years of roleplaying I'll be playing a female character, an ex forensic agent Astor (tremere internal afairs police) in Buenos Aires set in the 90', I'll tell you about my experience later but i would like to start the discution of playing oposite gendered characters (inspired by a recent video by The Gentleman Gamer on a similar topic) the ST of the game ill be playing said it was very hard and </span></span>tireing(not in a &quot;boring&quot; kind of tireing mind you)<span style=\"color:#444444\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"> but the other player said that it was not so, as the longest running character he had was a female and he never felt that way.<br /> <br /> What are your opinions and/or personal experience on the subject? (forgive if i make mistakes, its late and im not a native speaker of english)</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/122-talum","name":"Talum","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar122_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-25T20:03:14-08:00","id":79895,"title":"","content":"I'm not very good at it in most contexts.<br /> <br /> I don't set any rules on it besides &quot;I will stop it if you're being really stupid with it.&quot; But that's pretty much a standard rule for me in general, it just bears stressing because it can be a sensitive/trigger subject. Some people are really good at playing genders that are not their own; I don't see why it would be tiring for them to do that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-25T20:08:48-08:00","id":79898,"title":"","content":"My experience as an ST on an online game is that it's not really very difficult. That might change face to face; some people, myself included, probably do better in a purely textual medium than with speaking, which may have to do more with feeling uncomfortable with speaking. This goes along with my experience in writing, since all you need to be mindful of for any character is the set of individual quirks and opinions that each of them will have. Let the characterization lead you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-26T11:47:47-08:00","id":80409,"title":"","content":"(For context, I'm female)<br /> <br /> I've done it a number of times. I default to playing female characters, but have no qualms about playing men if there is a reason for me to do so.<br /> <br /> Most of my experiencing playing male characters comes from showing up to convention-style games where the GM has a pile of pre-generated characters to choose from. Sometime the characters are all male, so there's no choice (I have some issues with this, but that's a whole other thread). Other times, the character that sounds most fun to me just happens to male. In either case, I'm roleplaying. My character isn't me, and part of the fun in roleplay is being a different person in a different time and place.<br /> <br /> That said, gender identity is something very deeply entwined within most of us, so I understand when some of my peers are uncomfortable or unwilling to play outside their own gender. Pushing boundaries can be fun in a game, but no one should have to go beyond what's enjoyable for them.<br /> <br /> I've heard people complain about negative experiences with cross-gender play. Some are &quot;I tried it and didn't like it&quot;, which is fine. Most, however, follow the pattern of a man who plays a female character just so he can describe her physical attractiveness and narrate her doing things that are sexually suggestive or outright sexual. This might also happens with female players and male characters (or same-sex pairings, but this is the thread about cross-gendered play), but I haven't been privy to the sob stories. Unless the roleplay group has designated itself as &quot;The Sexual Fantasy Description Club&quot; this sort of thing is thing degrading, and disrespectful to everyone involved. It's not a problem with playing cross-gender. It's problem with players acting in grossly inappropriate ways.<br /> <br /> <br /> Only once have I deliberately created a male character. It was for a Dark Ages: Vampire campaign, so we were using a historical setting in which gender roles were a big deal. The ST pointed out that this matters far less, if at all, amongst Cainites. That's true, and especially so when the ST declares it, but it still mattered a hell of a lot amongst the regular humans of the day. That means a character's gender is going to be very defining for the kinds of backgrounds they might have prior to Embrace, and how they can interact with mortals. My character concept worked better as a man, so that's what I played.<br /> <br /> It was a good experience overall, and I really liked the character. The only annoyance was pronoun use that the table. Sometimes my character, Severyn, would called &quot;she&quot;. I don't think he would've liked that. It's not a big deal, though, and I really can't fault my friends for it. We're very used to using pronouns that match the gender we physically see on the person. Also, during a tabletop game you sometimes refer to the character, and sometimes to the player. Severyn will talk to the constable, but Jessa must make the roll to see how convincing Severyn's lie is. As long as people get the pronouns right most of the time while speaking in-character it's easy to overlook the rest of the confusion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\"><b>Demon Cat</b></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://flamingporcupine.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Illuminated Dark Ages character sheets and more</a>","edited":{"userid":10,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10-demon-cat","user":"Demon Cat","date":". 02-26-2014, 02:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10-demon-cat","name":"Demon Cat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"266"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-26T13:10:24-08:00","id":80448,"title":"","content":"Exelent post Demon Cat! Also you reminded me that i had played two female characters in the past, it was in conventions in a senario as the one you described, thats why i had forgotten. One I played as male because the miniature had realistic armor(it was a half elf paladin in D&amp;D) and didn't realize it was a she until i read the sheet a week later, and the other one was a gnome bard (again D&amp;D, there are no WoD games where i live <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )so i just played it as a child with the mouth of a sailor (4th edition, insults did more damage than my +2 bow, go figure)<br /> <br /> PS: can i use the name &quot;The Sexual Fantasy Description Club&quot; for a movie? (im a film student) sounds like there is a hilariously disturbing movie in there","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/122-talum","name":"Talum","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar122_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-26T14:54:30-08:00","id":80528,"title":"","content":"I don't personally do it a lot, however I consider that my limitation. When I've tried to play female characters before, they don't usually feel quite right, like I'm not quite doing them justice. It's very difficult not to fall into stereotypes. I now only play female characters if the story I want to tell absolutely cannot be told any other way, and then I'm very careful and watch myself constantly.<br /> <br /> I had one ST who ran our games for a few years who had seen so many bad examples of this that he outright banned it. His players were forbidden from playing different than their own gender and sexual orientation because it is difficult to maintain role-play for the rest of the group when one person can't understand their own character. I see his point, but would not go that extreme personally.<br /> <br /> However, I've seen people pull it off flawlessly. My suggestion as an ST is to keep an eye on it, particularly with role-players you don't know, and be prepared to tell them they need to make a new character if it gets bad, insulting, or disruptive, but if they are pulling it off, then there is no problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"558"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-27T02:01:10-08:00","id":80920,"title":"","content":"I have, generally, tended to prefer playing female characters over the years (I am a het guy IRL) and honestly I can\u2019t tell you why. But then I\u2019ve always liked hanging out with women more than guys, and always sought out women to rp with whenever I could, hoping I\u2019d get more rp and less hack &amp; slash. (I used to try and find actors for the same reason!) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> These tendencies have lessened as I have gotten older; I like to think maybe I\u2019ve grown a little out of stereotypes about how men and women act, but I don\u2019t know. I still play both.<br /> <br /> And I will completely agree with Saur Ops that it\u2019s a ton easier to do online. Though maybe not as hard in person as it once was.<br /> <br /> How\u2019s that for a non-answer? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u200b<span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\">Dante \u201cOwns-All-the-Books\u201d Laughing-Coyote<br /> <a href=\"https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/91390940/Werewolf%20the%20Apocalypse%20Reference.xlsx\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf (and Human) Reference Excel Spreadsheet</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/clouddrive/share?s=VoD6R2UDSGwijaft3H4kbM\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img593/9752/w20supporterhiddengarou.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img546/6111/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img31/2512/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1765-rderekp","name":"rderekp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-27T02:13:39-08:00","id":80923,"title":"","content":"I tend to play female characters in online games. I'm also someone who has more female friends than male friends.<br /> <br /> I think I prefer playing female characters because it allows a degree of separation from myself. One of my gaming pet peeves is players whose characters are idealized versions of themselves and who consequently take everything that happens to their characters extremely personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/260-chase-variant","name":"Chase Variant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar260_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-27T08:52:37-08:00","id":81073,"title":"","content":"The only place I really DON'T want to allow cross gender rp is in LARPs. Some suspension of disbelief is just too much to handle for me.<br /> <br /> Other than that, man, woman or Tzimisce, play whatever you want in my games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"558"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-27T19:19:23-08:00","id":81363,"title":"","content":"For me when it comes to myself making a character's gender doesn't really cross my mind. When I make a character I ether do it where I've thought about what I want them to be like, their powers, their motivations and so on for a while, or I just spill out a character out of quick inspiration. (The second don't tend to last more than a month at which point I get board of them). I don't really have a preference over male or female it's whatever I find fits the character. One reason why i will more then likely never play in a game where I have to use a premade. Unfortunately I do tend to stick to two female archetypes of ether the strong independent women or the fun gogeter party girl, which I REALLY need to get out of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2256-goblin404","name":"Goblin404","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2256_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-05T11:05:19-08:00","id":84721,"title":"","content":"When I LARP, I play characters of my own masculine gender. As a result, when I get a chance to play a tabletop or online game, I often find myself defaulting to a female PC just for diversity. The character's gender isn't really a bit issue for me. People are people, and nothing about being a woman changes the core drives and goals that people have. If there's one glitch in my playing of female PCs, though, it's that I'm godawful at playing a straight woman (or a gay man, for that matter). Sexuality and romance aren't things I delve into with characters too much anyway, but it just disappears from characters of mine that aren't interested in women. It's generally not a big deal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1812-deflare","name":"Deflare","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1812_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"90"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-05T12:58:47-08:00","id":84793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Deflare</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders?p=84721#post84721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I LARP, I play characters of my own masculine gender. As a result, when I get a chance to play a tabletop or online game, I often find myself defaulting to a female PC just for diversity.</div> </div> </div> </div>My husband and I had been LARPing for a few years. After we moved away the first character he created for a table-top game was female for exactly that same reason; he <i>couldn't</i> play a woman in LARP.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\"><b>Demon Cat</b></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://flamingporcupine.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Illuminated Dark Ages character sheets and more</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10-demon-cat","name":"Demon Cat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"266"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-05T14:32:47-08:00","id":84854,"title":"","content":"For context, I'm a 30-year-old, cis, hetero male.<br /> <br /> I'm currently a player in a <i>W:tA</i> game in which I play a 17-year-old female homid Black Fury Ahroun. It's definitely challenging, but I did it because a) it's something very different; b) it's challenging; c) Critical race and gender theory fascinate me, and I thought it would be interesting to explore many of these concepts anew by trying to put myself in a Black Fury head space. My storyteller is not sexist, but there's definitely a great deal of in-game sexism thrown at my character by NPCs and the like -- and I actually appreciate that. Part of playing a woman, I would imagine, is having to deal with the oppressive structures that come part and parcel with a heavily patriarchal society. If every NPC I met treated me with the same deference and respect as they would a man, I think it would serve as a disservice to the character and story, and further, be an act of sexism of itself (because we, as players, would be asked to believe that sexism isn't a thing in this World of Darkness, despite the fact that it is very much a thing in the real world, and that the World of Darkness is supposed to be a grimier, darker, generally more awful place to exist within).<br /> <br /> That said, my character is vain, and has a compulsion that forces her to look at herself, if only briefly, in every mirror or highly polished surface she encounters (she tries to hide this), and is constantly primping and trying to move this lock of hair just so, or adjuster her outfit, or what have you. In truth, I took this from my own personality (I don't look at myself in the mirror compulsively <i>exactly</i>, but I'm definitely overly concerned with my appearance). I recognized that this was a sort of a dangerous turn to take, as women (especially teenaged women, which is what my character is) are often accused of being overly vain, shallow, and obsessed with looks. That said, Rather than construct some uber-lady who utterly defied every stereotype, I acknowledged that this is a flaw that any human being can have, and gave it to her -- thereby also providing some excellent roleplaying opportunities when someone comments on how the ahroun might be too concerned with how she looks to really get into the fight and do the dirty, awful work required of that auspice.<br /> <br /> The one cheap avenue I took (in my opinion) is that she has a hatred of &quot;slavers&quot;, i.e., people who enslave others (per the U.N.'s definition of the same). I say it's cheap because it's almost as predictable as a female character having a hatred of rapists. That said, when another player pointed out that it was cheap, not because it was obvious, but because &quot;where are there slavers today?&quot;, I pointed out that they're everywhere, and was quickly able to substantiate that claim by finding some statistics on modern human trafficking -- which are staggering and sad. That said, as I see it, I had a friend and fellow player who was not entirely aware of how large a problem this is, and really only found out when he did because I had to be like, &quot;no, dude, it's a serious problem, even and <i>especially </i>in this country&quot;, when discussing this character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 03-05-2014, 05:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-06T09:35:17-08:00","id":85405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Deflare</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders?p=84721#post84721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If there's one glitch in my playing of female PCs, though, it's that I'm godawful at playing a straight woman (or a gay man, for that matter). Sexuality and romance aren't things I delve into with characters too much anyway, but it just disappears from characters of mine that aren't interested in women. It's generally not a big deal.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am somewhat the same, I can play a female or male character but when romance touches the story I kinda shut off, since I am generally a shy person face to face. Its even worse when the romance scene happens when I am playing a character of the opposite gender.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2448-dante","name":"Dante","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-07T07:14:01-08:00","id":85989,"title":"","content":"I have no problem playing female characters -- I've played them for 25+ years now, with nary an issue with other players (only once, with one gaming group I played with for a year, was there an &quot;issue&quot;, which only involved some of the players referring to my character by my gender pronouns -- &quot;he&quot;, &quot;his&quot; -- they just kept slipping-up perception-wise because their seeing me overrode their sense of my character's gender; I was playing a more subdued, laid-back woman, so the gender distinction wasn't prominent). In fact, I'd say my roster of characters is pretty much 50-50 split between male/female (with a few characters being genderless, due to their innate natures/origins, thrown in). Currently, I am playing two games, once a month for the past two years, with the &quot;A&quot; game being in the morning/most of the afternoon (MSH, where I play a woman; in fact, she's the same heroine I played in the same friend's game from over 20 years ago), and the &quot;B&quot; game afterwards (WoD), where I play a male. (Interesting gender-trait swap: Americanna is a Captain America/Wonder Woman pastiche who is physically powerful and the field leader of her team, while Andreas is a mage who, while physically in shape/above average capability, has no general combat abilities and isn't aggressive or good in close-combat situations. It's interesting playing a female in a &quot;traditionally male&quot; role then afterwards playing a male who isn't the stereotypical combat-monster that can creep up in WoD -- or any game setting, really -- but is more reserved/non-aggressive; it's also fun because the two characters share a few emotional/perceptual traits in common -- though they are simultaneously very different in many other ways -- and it's nice to see those similarities played in very different, albeit subtly so, ways...). I like playing a diverse range of personalities/characters, and gender (for me, at least) is just one more facet of creating/playing a given character. Back in the day, it helped out in some of my earlier groups that I had no issue playing a female because it lent itself to a more credible mix of characters, particularly because I play all of my characters &quot;legit&quot;: when I play a female, I don't play a caricature of one (except sometimes in humor games, like Teenagers or Paranoia, where such is par-for-the-setting), but a woman with real, non-exaggerated feminine traits (I've been complimented by a number of players/GMs over the past 3 decades that I do female characters very well, the way a female player would likely do). <br /> <br /> It boils down, ultimately, to how comfortable a player is with playing an opposite-gender character and how accepting/mature the group he plays with is. Per my above early-on gaming: when one has a &quot;sausage-fest&quot; of players (as was very often the case back in the 80's/much of the 90's), it is good to have a guy or two who will play a female character &quot;fo' realz&quot; so as to add a more credible dimensionality to the group dynamics; on the flip, it's cool to have a female player play a male character, as it broadens the range of what she might want to portray/act-out, legitimately, in many settings, w/o having to artificially make &quot;Politically Correct&quot; a given situation (she can play the captain of a WWII military unit, or the baron of a medieval land, sans the post-modern conceit of her *having* to play a woman who &quot;defies all convention&quot; for the time period in question).<br /> <br /> That's how I see it, in any case...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-07T16:43:26-08:00","id":86365,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So throwing fireballs at vampires while riding a dragon is fine, but gender equality is too unrealistic, and ruins the veracity of the setting with post-modern conceits?</div> </div> </div> </div>As a female, yes, it can. In fact, it can be extremely bothersome as it basically shoehorns a female character into such a role because the person playing the character is female and for no other reason. That is not a good thing, because it basically says that 'hey, a girl playing, gotta change history so she can play the army commander she wants'. And that is putting the player's gender before the player's ability to play.<br /> <br /> Just because your gender has been oppressed and been seen as lesser through history, doesn't mean you cannot understand or respect historical accuracy of a setting and want it. Being limited to what you can play in a WW2 game because of your gender despite your favourite character type being mostly seen as male is bad, but it's even worse when the otherwise historically accurate setting is changed solely so you could play that character type is even worse. It's telling the female player that they are different from other players and things have to be changed to fit them in a game.<br /> <br /> There are female players who absolutely want to play a muscly macho man who might be misgyonistic simply to explore this kind of person, or just have fun and laugh at their own character. Because it is not real, it's just pretend and fun. And being told -your- pretend is not acceptable because of gender equality,well..that comes across as patronizing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T03:43:47-08:00","id":86651,"title":"","content":"As Loxo said in post #20.<br /> It depends on the group, levels of suspension of disbelief, the groups preferences, the setting, the group, verisimilitude, and the individuals in the group. Role-play, in any context is about expression, and everyone's expressive ability, personal expression styles, personal expression levels, and social comfort levels with various topics vary greatly.<br /> <br /> The Gentleman's Agreement comes into play where you're all there to have fun, and each person needs to respect that. If you can't have fun, because of differences in play-style, talk to your group, or else find a new group.<br /> <br /> More on-topic: The DM / GM / ST often has to go in-character as various genders (in WoD, Tzimisce make Genderqueer much more common). So why does it matter if the players do too? So long as it is done well, anyway.<br /> <br /> Personally, I am the DM / GM / ST often enough that it doesn't matter. Our group has a couple of people that can RP as either gender well (whether that means respectfully, or as an ironic c<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\">aricature </span></span>depends on game and story, playing opposite gender well means very different things when playing Mage versus Toon).<br /> That said, being Trans*, I play what I damned well please, setting permitting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i>&quot;Maturity is knowing you were an idiot in the past.&quot;<br /> &quot;If that's true, then Wisdom is knowing you'll be an idiot in the future.&quot;<br /> &quot;And Common Sense is knowing that you should try not to be an idiot NOW.&quot;<br /> -Questionable Content 976</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1042-zhyzhaksmate","name":"ZhyzhaksMate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1042_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T03:53:37-08:00","id":86655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Edit: I am checking on permission to link to a blog post on the topic. If people are going to start their posts with, &quot;As a female&quot; in order to boost their credibility, I've got a few women I'd like to quote on the subject. Until I get that permission, suffice it to say that Ana, your view is far, <i>far</i> from unanimously held. It's almost like one woman can't speak for all women. Fuckin' crazy, huh?</div> </div> </div> </div>I like to apologise for these women who watched this thread and were offended by my post.<br /> <br /> What I meant by my post was, that player's gender shouldn't decide what they play in what setting and there shouldn't be changes to a setting to make room for a female in an odd job for the setting, if the female player doesn't want that. That is it. However, I admit I came across as self-righteous and I'd like to apologise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T06:24:30-08:00","id":86710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, I would reiterate the sentiment in one of the linked blog posts; there's rarely a good reason for a <b>fictional </b>setting to apply gender bias as a default. Furthermore, many game lines don't actually describe one as existing. Players tend to fill that in as a result of some persistent, inadequately accurate, nebulous ideas about &quot;the past.&quot; The ones that do give a reason for it may or may not be giving one that is worth paying attention to, depending on the highly subjective preferences of the players in a specific group.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's rarely a good reason for a <i>real</i> place to apply gender bias either, so I don't understand where you're going with that line.<br /> &quot;Worth paying attention&quot; is not going to be about morality, fairness or anything. It's worth so if the whole group thinks it'll be interesting. It's hard to have that happen when so much seems to be taboo.<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The upside of this thread and all the mention of World War 2, I have been invited to play a game, where all the players are members of the Night Witches (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_Witches\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_Witches</a>). Although I honestly think it's because a couple of the &quot;women can't be military commanders&quot; comments really pissed off the historian who read the thread and took umbrage at the idea that women were incapable of being anything fun until after the 1920 adoption of the 19th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lydia_Litvyak\" target=\"_blank\">Lydia Litvyak</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":32,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","user":"Synapse","date":". 03-08-2014, 09:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T13:34:03-08:00","id":86974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's a counterpoint to the frequent contention that gender bias is &quot;realistic&quot; for game settings. The argument that was made by Just John and Ana Mizuki to explain why they wanted female characters to face role restrictions that were not faced by male characters in their RPGs.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>There is a difference between putting a restriction on what a female character can do in a RP, and putting restriction to what sort of RP a female player can be part of. It is the latter rather than the former which my post was about, and even then it was about it being a bad thing for the female player. About respecting what the female player wants to play, rather than assuming what they want out of a game.<br /> <br /> I do not find gender bias a thing that absolutely has to exist in a game, especially in the fantasy game scenario that you used as an example. However, there a settings where addressing it can serve the female character well as a show how awesome they are for overcoming the bias. Or how they used it to their advantage to get things they want. Should it limit what a female character should and can do? Absolutely not. If a female or male player playing a female character wants to explore it and is comfortable with it, I see no problem having it in my games. And even then, it is not restricting what the female character can do, but using the -perceived- bias as a way for the female character to rise above what society thinks of them.<br /> <br /> However, if a player is not OK with having the bias, well, then there is no bias in the game. Like many have here said, it is all about what the groups and players want in a game.<br /> <br /> I hope this explains what I meant.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T15:09:29-08:00","id":87032,"title":"","content":"We have rather frequent cross gender play, from both sexes. I often play females, but I also feel as much female as male (not much more maybe slightly) and TTRPGs are a pretty good way to live that out, as I am IRL physically male and OK with that, but really enjoy playing females in video games and TTRPGs, especially ones with slice of life aspects to satisfy the other part of my psyche. I'd say I play ~2/3 female but not exclusively. Currently all 9 games but one have at least player cross gender playing, and about half include a female playing a male.<br /> <br /> The main thing is it should be a character first, not a gender first. If their personality is just that they are a girl/guy there is a problem. I have had people who were problematic playing the opposite sex (or gay characters) but I generally dealt with it by dealing with them individually. Some players play opposite gender habitually, some rarely do. I know both women and men who run about 50/50. For us it is not a big deal; men and women are people, after all, and most of us have more in common with, say, a 21st century person of the opposite sex than a 10th century person of the same.<br /> <br /> For me a major thing is if say a male is playing a female, is he doing it to have this sexy plaything, or is he identifying as her for purposes of the game (as much as he does other characters).<br /> <br /> As for sex/romance, many of our games have a lot of it, but it does not tend to be X-rated (usually). We fade to black and such. But intercharacter relationships are a major thing, sex happens and is sometimes a major motivation for characters of either sex, but it tends to be more HBO Original Movie/Series than Cinimax After Dark. Most of us are married (or have been single for a long time) so there is not a lot of &quot;doing stupid things for love&quot; in our lives, so it can be kinda fun to have such in the games.<br /> <br /> Gender bias depends on the wants and needs of the group, as does level of homophobia and/or transphobia and/or racism. In general, most games I run are real world set (currently 4/5 are within 3 years of present) and nominally ape real world levels of each, though in general it is not present if players do not want to have to deal with it, especially those who would be impacted IC. It is also more likely to come up in, say, Vampire or Geist than say Scion.<br /> <br /> Pretty much all our groups games are mixed with at least two males and two females in the game (only one of 9 games currently going is all male, and only one other only has one female, and I am not personally in either of those though not for that reason). I think our comfort with romance and cross gender play may be partially for that reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T17:08:22-08:00","id":87092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">t's a counterpoint to the frequent contention that gender bias is &quot;realistic&quot; for game settings. The argument that was made by Just John and Ana Mizuki to explain why they wanted female characters to face role restrictions that were not faced by male characters in their RPGs.<br /> <br /> It is something that is brought up fairly often and it is, frankly, a profoundly oblivious position to take up when discussing a game line that deviates sharply from reality in many ways (as the overwhelming majority do). Playing a game about cowboys fighting zombies, and then declaring that women can only be saloon girls and school marms, because otherwise it is not a realistic portrayal of the American frontier is pretty fucking stupid on about six different levels.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think there is anything in your rhetoric to which I take exception, or with which I vehemently disagree. That is, I agree that we should run/play the games that most give rise to people having fun, do not make anyone uncomfortable and/or marginalize anyone, and ultimately fulfill the goals (thematic and otherwise) that the game participants (player and ST/DM/GM) have agreed upon.<br /> <br /> That said, I would argue (and I don't think this necessarily run contrary to what you're saying) that certain settings militate that there be some sexism. Werewolf in particular (and especially if it is a historical setting) has an entire tribe of Garou, the existence of which, and whose philosophy, are largely informed by and are a reaction to the historical fact of sexism. The Black Furies are, in addition to protecting the <i>Wyld places</i> (which may or may not be the result of some kind of sexist, coded language stereotyping), a push-back against the pervasive patriarchy that has existed, probably, <i>forever</i> (if you believe Catharine A. MacKinnon).<br /> <br /> With that in mind, I could see there being modern Garou septs, packs, Caerns, what have you, in which there is no sexism and everyone is a feminist and/or feminist ally (I added <i>ally</i> because there are those who believe that men cannot be feminists -- as a man, I will not opine as to that particular matter). However, and particularly in historical settings, it would seem disingenuous to have a Black Fury in a game without there being some kind of attempt by the setting characters (NPCs, what have you) to disadvantage women. My point is probably best illustrated by the fact of the existence of the Black Fury gift, &quot;Man's Skin&quot;, that enables female Furies to appear male; this gift only exists as a reaction to the fact that women have been disadvantaged, and &quot;other&quot;, in most societies of which we have a record, practically since time immemorial. This is an incredibly pragmatic gift, and while it appears in W20, it first appeared (I believe) in either the Dark Ages or Wild West game settings. While I am not prepared to say that anyone <i>must</i> run their game one way or another, I <i>will</i> say that having a Black Fury in a game without a patriarchy or any structural or systemic sexism, is roughly equivalent to portraying a setting with a &quot;Resistance&quot; faction, but no oppressive authoritarian proto-fascist regime to combat; like <i>Rage </i>without<i> The</i> <i>Machine</i>. Mind you, I'm not saying that there can be no Black Furies without sexism, so much as I'm saying that Black Furies without sexism are not really the Black Furies from the setting materials.<br /> <br /> That said, and with regards to something you said earlier in the thread,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loxosceles</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other thing... is that some of my players do experience the real world side of that on a regular basis. It is something that they have been hurt by (to varying degrees), something that impacts their daily life, something that they have trouble <i>getting away from</i>. They don't want to have it in their relaxing fun time game activity, realistic, hyper-realistic, or even just once in awhile. They live it on a daily basis, and no matter what the game is, no matter what other subjects it explores, even if it's a gritty, dark, dramatic, tense WoD setting, they don't actually need any more of it than they already get.</div> </div> </div> </div> I would have to agree that, if a particular aspect of the game is going to be hurtful to, or detract from someone's overall enjoyment of the game, then there is absolutely no legitimate reason to include it in any game. And if the intellectual dishonesty (assuming there is any) of running a game without any sexism is entirely too much for the other players to handle, then I think you either play another game or find different players. None of this stuff (roleplaying games) is ever going to be worth hurting someone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 03-08-2014, 08:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-09T23:56:02-07:00","id":88000,"title":"","content":"To date, I'm 60% male, 40% female character. What's really nice is that I can do a female voice and it actually sounds female. It's very useful and creeps people the heck out. It generally works very well though and doesn't disrupt play. It also helps cause the others can tell when I'm in RP mode and when I'm in OOC as the switch is apparent.<br /> <br /> For those that can't do it, well I don't know what to say. Good luck though. I've got no problems with it, but I recommend not doing it for your first time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-13T00:49:20-07:00","id":90021,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, I would argue (and I don't think this necessarily run contrary to what you're saying) that certain settings militate that there be some sexism. Werewolf in particular (and especially if it is a historical setting) has an entire tribe of Garou, the existence of which, and whose philosophy, are largely informed by and are a reaction to the historical fact of sexism. The Black Furies are, in addition to protecting the <i>Wyld places</i> (which may or may not be the result of some kind of sexist, coded language stereotyping), a push-back against the pervasive patriarchy that has existed, probably, <i>forever</i> (if you believe Catharine A. MacKinnon).<br /> <br /> With that in mind, I could see there being modern Garou septs, packs, Caerns, what have you, in which there is no sexism and everyone is a feminist and/or feminist ally (I added <i>ally</i> because there are those who believe that men cannot be feminists -- as a man, I will not opine as to that particular matter). However, and particularly in historical settings, it would seem disingenuous to have a Black Fury in a game without there being some kind of attempt by the setting characters (NPCs, what have you) to disadvantage women. My point is probably best illustrated by the fact of the existence of the Black Fury gift, &quot;Man's Skin&quot;, that enables female Furies to appear male; this gift only exists as a reaction to the fact that women have been disadvantaged, and &quot;other&quot;, in most societies of which we have a record, practically since time immemorial. This is an incredibly pragmatic gift, and while it appears in W20, it first appeared (I believe) in either the Dark Ages or Wild West game settings. While I am not prepared to say that anyone <i>must</i> run their game one way or another, I <i>will</i> say that having a Black Fury in a game without a patriarchy or any structural or systemic sexism, is roughly equivalent to portraying a setting with a &quot;Resistance&quot; faction, but no oppressive authoritarian proto-fascist regime to combat; like <i>Rage </i>without<i> The</i> <i>Machine</i>. Mind you, I'm not saying that there can be no Black Furies without sexism, so much as I'm saying that Black Furies without sexism are not really the Black Furies from the setting materials. </div> </div> </div>This. Exactly this.<br /> <br /> I personally refuse to compromise my game settings for the sake of modern political correctness. I don't care if my players want to play opposite genders, sexual preferences or anything else as long as they do it well and believably, but I run a lot of historical games and I think it does a horrendous disservice to the game and world integrity if I am running a game in Georgian England and have women military commanders sailing off to fight the rebellious colonists, or women sitting in the House of Lords. It utterly compromises the setting integrity and makes me feel like I'm running a revisionist history joke.<br /> <br /> You ask why a game with fantastical elements needs to have racism/sexism/other prejudice involved? I'll counter by asking why anyone who is voluntarily playing a game of horror where they portray bloodsucking monsters who regularly do appalling things, or shapechanging beasts intent to fight the spread of human domination, or Awakened Godlings searching for a greater truth in the midst of the mundane really need to be protected from the bad things that exist in this world like prejudice? WoD is a horror setting, specifically focused on highlighting the ugliest aspects of the world, very much including prejudice. My personal policy is to have such things at the historically appropriate level for the game I'm running, but to bring them to the forefront when and if it serves the story.<br /> <br /> Now when running in <i>fantasy</i> settings it is a different story. When the setting is entirely fictional, I can set my own levels of such things without any concern for historical integrity (I say integrity rather than accuracy because I am NOT a history professor and make no claims to knowing THE TRUTH, but rather establish a truth that feels right for the setting based on our common understanding of the era in question). When running games in fictional settings they can have whatever levels of prejudice feel right and fit both the setting I want to create and the mood of the game in question. Prejudice is an inherently human trait, and I think ignoring it for the sake of a modern liberal sense of political correctness does a great disservice to my game and my players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"<a href=http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png target=_blank>http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png</a>\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2323-killersquirrel59","name":"killersquirrel59","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"558"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-13T01:42:12-07:00","id":90047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>theoutlander523</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders?p=88000#post88000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's really nice is that I can do a female voice and it actually sounds female. It's very useful and creeps people the heck out. It generally works very well though and doesn't disrupt play. It also helps cause the others can tell when I'm in RP mode and when I'm in OOC as the switch is apparent.<br /> <br /> For those that can't do it, well I don't know what to say. Good luck though. I've got no problems with it, but I recommend not doing it for your first time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have a fairly deep voice, and whenever I attempt a female one, it invariably comes off as cheesy and weird. I don't let that hold me back from having female NPCs though. If my players can imagine themselves being undead monsters who can summon tentacles of pure darkness, they can bloody well imagine my voice being feminine. Hrmf.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/300-hod","name":"HOD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar300_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"466"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T03:57:34-07:00","id":93359,"title":"","content":"I do not have a problem with my players doing it and as the person who is usually the GM/ST I have to play female characters anyway. Whenever I am player I simply ask myself: &quot;Would this concept be enhanced/enabled by playing a female?&quot; if the answer is yes, I do that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T11:33:19-07:00","id":93623,"title":"","content":"For my group, a man playing a female character is practically default. Most of my players will say specifically that they find it easier to make interesting female characters rather than male characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T12:26:28-07:00","id":93695,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders?p=93623#post93623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For my group, a man playing a female character is practically default. Most of my players will say specifically that they find it easier to make interesting female characters rather than male characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thats, very strange, any idea whys that?<br /> <br /> Also i forgot to say, my personal experience with the Tremere character i made went great but i havent played her since and want to have at least two more games playing her before i tell in detail how it went","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/122-talum","name":"Talum","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar122_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T20:35:59-07:00","id":94053,"title":"","content":"My thought is that we all draw from the same anime club, and in anime female characters are usually given personality while male characters are typically made bland as possible so they can be made into self-insert fantasy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-19T14:42:31-07:00","id":94475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/79886-on-playing-oposite-genders?p=94053#post94053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My thought is that we all draw from the same anime club, and in anime female characters are usually given personality while male characters are typically made bland as possible so they can be made into self-insert fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Well, how about that?<br /> <br /> I can kind of see the issue, but is that not more reason to invent your own interesting male characters?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-20T00:38:25-07:00","id":94764,"title":"","content":"I don't think fiction in general is lacking enough for interesting male characters that my tiny little game group needs to pitch into the war effort.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T13:35:13-07:00","id":145984,"title":"","content":"I try to go fifty fifty when it comes to gender ratio. With occassional genderless entity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T15:20:08-07:00","id":146089,"title":"","content":"\u200bFor context, I was born female, but identify agender with a slight male leaning. I perceive gender in the modern era to be outdated and don't particularly view gender as being tied to one's personality at all. However, I also understand that other people, especially in earlier historical contexts, do view their own and others' genders as important as it impacts their perspective on others. I feel very comfortable playing any gender I wish, but I do often have difficulty accommodating a more common perception of gender. I'll admit, I tend to use male characters more often than females, probably 55-45 split or so. But to me, I focus more on who the character is than on what genitals they were born with, which can be an issue if I want to play a character where these things matter. I'm improving, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T18:31:42-07:00","id":146236,"title":"","content":"I, on the other hand, was born male but identify as... more feminine than masculine, though not necessarily female. I'll admit I don't like playing &quot;male&quot; characters. Generally female, though if I had a Tzimisce it would most likely make heavy, repeated use of Vicissitude. I briefly played a Wraith once that was born male and identified as female in life, to the extent that her corpus was such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1082-nfi","name":"NFI","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T22:27:14-07:00","id":146380,"title":"","content":"Some people are just better at it than others, frankly. I've played with gamers who, however well-intentioned, just can't play it convincingly and kind of blow the suspension of disbelief. I've also played with gamers who are so natural at it I never even question. A husband and wife duo I know are actually quite adept at playing characters of swapped genders and its made for some fun campaigns/chronicles.<br /> <br /> I've attempted playing female characters before (as a cis-hetero male), but I'm honestly just not that good at it. Even my female NPCs, while functional, are often not a roleplaying piece-de-resistance. This is unfortunate as I actually quite like writing for female characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-22T22:56:51-07:00","id":146438,"title":"","content":"I've played a pretty even split of characters as far as genders go. and sexual orientations. many with their own love lives, sometimes not just depends on the nature of the game in question. I've never had a prolem with crossing genders or orientations in games. though being a gay man in real life i guess i just find it easier to explore these themes than a lot of people. though i have been pleasantly surprised by how many people in other troupes also play cross gender as most of the people I've met face to face have all stuck almost entirely to their own gender and orientation. must be a small town Midwest thing. *shrugs* I guess that the best way to approach it is too not try to play a female character but a character. I ask myself what is that makes this person unique and how does her being a woman affect that? does it affect that at all? in some cases yes and in some cases no.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2708-lost_in_the_hedge777","name":"Lost_in_the_Hedge777","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2708_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T05:49:58-07:00","id":146613,"title":"","content":"Cisgendered hetero male.<br /> <br /> I've played female characters as often as males since I started RPing. It began by realizing someone needed to play the romantic interests of the heroes my friends were making, and reading a lot of the original Marvel <i>Excalibur</i> run. Good characters in there, three of them female (Kitty Pryde, Rachel Summer, Meggan). I quite enjoy playing female characters, and don't shy from romance. My best male friends and I have played out several decent romances between our characters. Good fun. <br /> <br /> Online, I've engaged in long term RPs as a female character, going into full sexual roleplaying as well. I've done the same as a male character. Doesn't bother me, as long as the person on the other end understood &quot;this is play&quot;.<br /> <br /> I have no issues with people wanting to play opposite gender characters when GMing, but have reserved the right to say &quot;no&quot; if they've proven to be poor at it (some people don't shift gender gears as easily). This &quot;no&quot; is relaxed every once in a while to give them a fresh chance.<br /> <br /> I have no issues with cross-gender play in LARP as long as it's (a) tastefully done (b) honest effort is made to look/behave/sound like the alternate gender. I've dealt with it three times in LARP, ranging from a good performance to cringe worthy.<br /> <br /> In my group males and female gamers alike dabble in the other gender, or play either equally, with only a few &quot;sticking to their own gender&quot;. I think all gamers should take a stab at it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3078-coffee-zombie","name":"Coffee Zombie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3078_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T08:43:30-07:00","id":146806,"title":"","content":"My future vampire PC is lesbian, but that is somewhat important by her origins, yet secondary in comparison to her non-sexuality related motives and aspects of personality. Not that it is relevant post-Embrace, she is practically now asexual because of losing sex drive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-23T11:38:29-07:00","id":146945,"title":"","content":"Gender is just one of the many things people in my neck of the woods play with. Not everyone does it, but most people are quite capable of it and welcome to do so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-27T12:13:39-07:00","id":150693,"title":"","content":"As a GM I've never given it much thought in the sense of seeing it as a big deal. I've been GMing games since the mid-90's (just a player before then), and in that time I've had to portray hundreds of NPCs (maybe more). Considering sex/gender is basically a 50/50 split, I've had to portray a lot of women, as well as characters of different racial or cultural backgrounds than myself. Comes with the territory. Now, a good deal of those female NPCs (I'm male) aren't highly detailed, due to being tertiary to the plot, and the roleplaying may be slightly glossed-over but that's no different than a good portion of the male NPCs who are equally tertiary to the story I'm telling. But if that female NPC is a main character, then yeah, I'll roleplay her fully to my players. In two cases, I've had players take the True Love Merit (one in a Dark Ages Vampire game from nearly 10 years ago, and one currently in a Renaissance Mage game) - in both those cases, their female love interests basically became GMPCs that feature pretty heavily in the campaign and so they're as &quot;fleshed-out&quot; as a long-term PC and I do my best to portray the actual love/romance between the characters. To my great satisfaction the Dark Ages Vampire game ran until Modern Nights (over 3.5 years of real-time) and so that relationship between my player's character and his love interest was portrayed over nearly a thousand years and when his True Love died during Gehenna, the player walked his own character into the sun during a wonderfully poignant moment (the player himself was actually weeping in real life [in a good way] - I consider it one of my greatest achievements in storytelling to date). So I like to think I do a decent job of portraying respectable and interesting female characters.<br /> <br /> I don't get a chance to play a lot of PCs since I'm the dedicated storyteller for our group so I make the important NPCs count. Out of the few PCs I have been able to run in long-term games (most of my PCs are usually only for one-shots), only one has been female. Though I have also played a gay male PC (I'm straight) in an ongoing Shadowrun game.<br /> <br /> As for the other subject that's cropped up on this thread - setting verisimilitude and roles for women - I'm in the camp of &quot;do what makes sense for the setting and the players&quot;. So, pretty much all fictional settings I run are fairly equal-opportunity for women (I do have a female player in my group - she's been with the group since its inception in the late 90's). However, to use my most current setting as an example, I don't shy away from historical accuracy for my Renaissance Mage game. And my female player is completely fine with that and sees it as perfectly reasonable. The key is to not go out of your way to actively make the setting punish the female character (or worse, the player) or remove the character's agency. The setting might be oppressive to women but it's also a bunch of other stuff - treat the setting like you would a character - you wouldn't make a character whose only trait is being gay (I hope) like some awful sitcom, but you could make a character who also happens to be gay. It's the same thing for a setting, the Renaissance setting is oppressive but that's not its only trait or even the most defining - that would be the hope and opportunity for endless potential that the era brings: endless potential for war, technology (the sky's the limit), but also endless potential for women and everyone else. So my female player is having a wonderful time playing a 16-year-old recently Awakened Mage who is trying to navigate her new found power while also dealing with her family trying to marry her off relatively soon as said family jostles for political position in the Italian city-states. Her father is more willing to allow her the freedom to roam and be herself; it's the character's mother who is dead-set on women's roles and marriage (the character, consequently gets along with her father much better than her mother, meanwhile her brother is also a bit of a tool). Most of this set-up was the player's idea and I'm just rolling with what she gave me. In the long run though, if her character decides to seek more political and worldly mortal power, the player understands (and is completely fine with) the conventions of the time and that she'll need to go the Catherine de Medici route, at least for the time being. But those same limitations aren't imposed on her when seeking political power in the world of Daedalean politics (she's an Artificer). Consequently, for anyone looking to run a game in the Renaissance (be it Mage or anything else) that involves courtly drama, for a good look behind the eyes of a woman from the times (well, late Middle Ages, rather) I recommend the works of Christine de Pizan.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I hope my examples and experiences, illustrate my thoughts on the matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3098-theblockknight","name":"TheBlockKnight","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-27T14:28:41-07:00","id":150791,"title":"","content":"As someone who's spent more time GM'ing than actually playing in games, I don't give it a thought. I gender characters however seems most appropriate, and play characters of any gender as seems most appropriate to the character. I <i>rarely</i> have characters (PC's or NPC's) in which gender is a central identity to the character, but I avoid it when and where possible.<br /> <br /> I just ask fellow players to not be stupid about it, just as I do any other aspect of characterization or character creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T20:25:35-07:00","id":152045,"title":"","content":"White Wolf Games have always been (by Tabletop or LARP standards) very inclusive to varying sexual and gender identities. I've seen people play same-gender and other-gender (there really isn't an &quot;opposite&quot; gender) characters, as well as characters with varying sexual leanings. In my experience it depends on the player. Some simply can't deny the urge to play it up in some uncomfortable fashion. Others I've played with do it so seamlessly you don't even notice that half (or more than half) of their character's gender and/or sexuality does not match there own. Though, in my personal experience it is harder for people to play differing sexuality than gender identity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1604-oliver-the-bold","name":"Oliver the Bold","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-05T10:10:21-07:00","id":160048,"title":"","content":"I tend to match my own gender, so mostly play men. I've played female characters once or twice at the tabletop--including for the Scion game that's coming up for my friends and I, but most of the players won't know that--but not too often; I've had the good fortune to share a gaming table with multiple women for years, so I have no particular desire to balance out genders around the table. My wife tends to jump that barrier more often than I do. On the other hand, I've also seen a few men around the table make female characters, and all three were bad; one was universally agreed upon by the actual women at the table to do a terrible job of it, one isn't inclined to, but did it when strictly necessary, and still couldn't pull it off to save his life--the first time he tried to voice a female NPC he did so in a squeaky falsetto that Monty Python would call painful and forced--and while I haven't heard actual critique of the latest one's efforts, it's his first tabletop RPG and his character is the sexy broody loner femme fatale type, while he clearly doesn't get any of the...conventions or social boundaries I'd consider common sense around the gaming table. That last part is easily a discussion for another time, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/561-mockery","name":"Mockery","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"876"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}